1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
A small town spontaneously combusts.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:13,000
A tanker explodes, taking lives and leaving ecological disaster in its wake.

3
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:18,000
A parking structure mysteriously collapses.

4
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
The dream project of a Soviet strongman turns into a nightmare.

5
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:25,000
And a cloud of gas leaves a landscape of death.

6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:28,000
Now, engineering disasters on modern marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,840
January 17, 2001 dawned chilly in the city of Hutchinson, Kansas.

8
00:00:45,680 --> 00:00:50,380
A town of 40,000 located near the geographical center of the United States.

9
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,720
Then, all hell broke loose.

10
00:00:53,721 --> 00:01:02,460
two buildings in the heart of town were slammed by an explosion and burst into flames the force

11
00:01:02,461 --> 00:01:08,460
of the explosion shattering glass a block away and the flames sending smoke twisting over the city

12
00:01:08,461 --> 00:01:17,040
one store manager will never forget that day the last thing i remember was like a rumble like

13
00:01:17,041 --> 00:01:24,740
thunder and I looked up and the ceiling tiles were all falling down and the next thing I knew

14
00:01:24,741 --> 00:01:31,481
I heard sirens. I was buried in it. When we came back we were still in shock.

15
00:01:31,482 --> 00:01:33,320
Couldn't believe that

16
00:01:33,321 --> 00:01:36,381
it had happened. Our building wasn't there.

17
00:01:36,382 --> 00:01:39,940
There was just a huge geyser of a fire just out of

18
00:01:39,941 --> 00:01:40,641
control.

19
00:01:40,642 --> 00:01:46,560
As firefighters worked to douse flames that reached as high as 70 feet, city officials

20
00:01:46,561 --> 00:01:49,320
could only guess at what might have caused the explosion and fire.

21
00:01:50,160 --> 00:01:53,720
We thought it was just a normal explosion due to, it was cold that day,

22
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:57,720
that maybe some furnace malfunctioned and there was a leak in the gas.

23
00:01:57,960 --> 00:02:02,100
But when officials turned off natural gas lines leading into downtown Hutchinson,

24
00:02:02,700 --> 00:02:05,220
the fires only seemed to burn higher and hotter.

25
00:02:05,760 --> 00:02:09,900
So we knew that we had something else that we had no control of

26
00:02:09,901 --> 00:02:11,480
and couldn't find out what was wrong.

27
00:02:11,481 --> 00:02:17,200
Into the evening, firefighters tried to quench the blaze that simply wouldn't go out.

28
00:02:19,180 --> 00:02:22,860
Then as dusk fell, several miles east of downtown,

29
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:28,580
30-foot-tall geysers of natural gas and salt water bubbled up out of the ground.

30
00:02:29,380 --> 00:02:32,760
It was a strange development, and it only added to the confusion.

31
00:02:33,780 --> 00:02:39,060
Because while it confirmed for authorities that there was a significant underground presence of natural gas,

32
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:43,140
No one knew where such massive amounts of the gas were coming from,

33
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:45,640
or where fire might strike next.

34
00:02:48,860 --> 00:02:50,800
Officials found out the next morning,

35
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:53,900
when another geyser bubbled up beneath the trailer park,

36
00:02:54,280 --> 00:02:56,040
found a spark, and exploded.

37
00:02:56,700 --> 00:02:59,080
Two people were killed in that trailer explosion.

38
00:02:59,740 --> 00:03:03,440
And that led to an evacuation area of about a two-mile area

39
00:03:03,441 --> 00:03:06,420
and a half-a-mile area of residential people.

40
00:03:06,940 --> 00:03:08,240
Then people began to panic.

41
00:03:08,241 --> 00:03:14,100
Schools were closed, mail was stopped at the area, and people wanted to know if things were under control.

42
00:03:14,560 --> 00:03:19,080
By that time, we had a declaration from the governor that we had a state of emergency.

43
00:03:19,660 --> 00:03:28,480
Kansas Gas Service, also known as KGAS, the local gas utility company, started to drill a series of 58 boreholes to try to vent the gas.

44
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:31,840
People in Hutchinson were afraid. They were scared to death.

45
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:36,840
They had no idea where the next geyser was going to come up, if their house was going to blow up.

46
00:03:36,841 --> 00:03:39,940
The boreholes were apparently effective in venting the gas pressure.

47
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:46,420
After the trailer park explosion on January 18th, there were no other explosions or fires in Hutchinson.

48
00:03:47,300 --> 00:03:54,080
But with two deaths and a city gripped by fear, officials were anxious to somehow establish a pattern to the explosions.

49
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:57,580
And their search took them back into Hutchinson's history.

50
00:03:57,581 --> 00:04:02,640
Beneath the city of Hutchinson lay a natural formation of salt

51
00:04:02,641 --> 00:04:07,180
A 250 million year old mineral deposit from the Permian age

52
00:04:07,181 --> 00:04:09,320
Called the Hutchinson Salt Member

53
00:04:09,321 --> 00:04:11,760
That's more than 400 feet thick

54
00:04:11,761 --> 00:04:14,580
And stretches through Kansas, Oklahoma and Texas

55
00:04:14,581 --> 00:04:17,040
The largest salt deposit in the U.S.

56
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:23,040
In the 1800s, businessmen started salt mines to extract the valuable mineral

57
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:27,820
And entrepreneurs made Hutchinson home to several European-style spas

58
00:04:27,821 --> 00:04:30,380
that featured mineral salt wells for their patrons.

59
00:04:31,840 --> 00:04:35,620
We went back and actually woke up the museum director

60
00:04:35,621 --> 00:04:38,380
and went back in the history books of the city when it was founded

61
00:04:38,381 --> 00:04:41,980
to see where all these salt companies were located.

62
00:04:42,380 --> 00:04:44,480
And they were located in the southern part of the town,

63
00:04:44,620 --> 00:04:46,120
and that's where we were finding the wells.

64
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:48,560
So we thought maybe there was some kind of resemblance.

65
00:04:48,561 --> 00:04:54,860
Investigators suspected that the old salt wells were providing a pathway for the gas to get to the surface

66
00:04:54,861 --> 00:04:58,800
But no one knew where the gas originated

67
00:04:58,801 --> 00:05:04,500
We were plotting the wells that were flaring up and to see if there was a pattern

68
00:05:04,501 --> 00:05:08,220
If they would form some kind of a line to show us where the source was at

69
00:05:08,221 --> 00:05:12,420
And it indicated to us it looked like there was a source maybe come from the west of town

70
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:17,860
Outside Hutchinson is the Yagi Gas Field, an underground storage facility

71
00:05:18,860 --> 00:05:25,180
The Yagi field is one of the hubs in the national gas storage and transportation system.

72
00:05:25,620 --> 00:05:29,400
There's less than, fewer than two dozen of these hubs around the country,

73
00:05:29,520 --> 00:05:32,440
so they're very critical parts of the natural gas infrastructure.

74
00:05:32,980 --> 00:05:38,400
Gas comes in by pipeline, and then it's sent from here out on distribution lines

75
00:05:38,401 --> 00:05:41,120
all over the Midwest and connecting up with the national grid.

76
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:47,140
The Yagi storage field, which holds about 3.5 billion cubic feet of gas,

77
00:05:47,141 --> 00:05:52,500
was first developed in the late 1970s to store liquid propane at low pressure.

78
00:05:54,020 --> 00:06:00,540
At that time, engineers created about 70 long-necked gas storage holding areas, known as jugs.

79
00:06:01,580 --> 00:06:06,740
To create a jug, engineers drill about 600 feet down into the salt deposit

80
00:06:06,741 --> 00:06:10,600
and then use water to dissolve the salt and make a man-made cavern.

81
00:06:11,320 --> 00:06:15,780
Then the water is pumped out, leaving a stable storage place that can be sealed

82
00:06:15,781 --> 00:06:17,540
by cementing around the well casing.

83
00:06:18,140 --> 00:06:22,460
These things are large enough to hold about 100,000 barrels of fluid,

84
00:06:22,900 --> 00:06:24,280
42 gallons per barrel.

85
00:06:24,380 --> 00:06:29,100
That's 4.2 million gallons of fluid in one of these jugs in the salt.

86
00:06:29,580 --> 00:06:31,020
Then, about a decade later,

87
00:06:31,640 --> 00:06:33,840
the company that made the jugs went out of business,

88
00:06:34,440 --> 00:06:36,700
and the local gas company converted the facility

89
00:06:36,701 --> 00:06:39,480
to hold natural gas at high pressure

90
00:06:39,481 --> 00:06:42,120
rather than liquid propane at lower pressure.

91
00:06:43,100 --> 00:06:44,740
The change was significant.

92
00:06:44,741 --> 00:06:50,640
because higher pressure storage requires that the jugs are perfectly sealed in order to prevent leaks.

93
00:06:52,540 --> 00:06:55,520
Investigators believe that just before the explosions in Hutchinson,

94
00:06:56,000 --> 00:07:00,060
there was a pressure drop in the gas stored in a jug designated S1.

95
00:07:01,320 --> 00:07:03,860
This suggested the gas had exited the jug,

96
00:07:04,300 --> 00:07:07,000
but investigators still needed to know where it went

97
00:07:07,001 --> 00:07:10,620
and how it might have traveled the eight miles to Hutchinson.

98
00:07:10,621 --> 00:07:17,300
To answer that question, geologists from the Kansas Geological Survey deployed what are

99
00:07:17,301 --> 00:07:21,980
known as seismic thumper trucks to create maps of underground conditions.

100
00:07:21,981 --> 00:07:28,300
A thumper truck works by literally thumping the ground to send shock waves into the earth.

101
00:07:28,301 --> 00:07:32,320
These are bounced back to the surface with varying degrees of intensity, depending upon

102
00:07:32,321 --> 00:07:35,480
the nature of the material they encounter below.

103
00:07:35,481 --> 00:07:40,060
This enabled scientists to make detailed maps of the nether regions between the city of

104
00:07:40,061 --> 00:07:43,680
Hutchinson and the Yaggy Field facility.

105
00:07:43,681 --> 00:07:47,800
Right here it looks fairly flat, but underground 600 feet the rocks are

106
00:07:47,801 --> 00:07:51,640
dipping and so they they slowly incline towards the city of Hutchinson.

107
00:07:51,641 --> 00:07:56,300
Natural gas gets out into those rocks, it comes out of the well, comes out of the

108
00:07:56,301 --> 00:08:00,580
the jug, it hits that rock layer and it wants to find the highest place, it wants

109
00:08:00,581 --> 00:08:04,080
to rise to the top. So it found this rock layer and found a pathway and just

110
00:08:04,081 --> 00:08:07,380
headed straight that way for eight miles right under downtown Hutchinson.

111
00:08:07,381 --> 00:08:13,860
Scientists generated geophysical maps of the underground pathway to validate their theory about the gas.

112
00:08:15,020 --> 00:08:19,240
They came to believe that the gas leak started in the S1 jug.

113
00:08:20,580 --> 00:08:24,780
To confirm this, investigators photographed the inside of the jug.

114
00:08:25,740 --> 00:08:29,920
They discovered that the steel casing that ran down into the salt cavern was damaged,

115
00:08:30,500 --> 00:08:33,460
allowing the high-pressure natural gas to escape into the ground.

116
00:08:33,461 --> 00:08:38,120
Investigators claim that even though there was a pressure drop at Yaggy Field,

117
00:08:38,760 --> 00:08:42,500
operators at the gas field either ignored it or didn't detect it

118
00:08:42,501 --> 00:08:46,340
and kept topping off the jug, keeping the high pressure constant.

119
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:52,720
While gas company engineers still seek other sources for the leak that ignited Hutchinson,

120
00:08:53,280 --> 00:08:58,020
most independent investigators agree that the Yaggy Field is the most likely origin.

121
00:08:58,021 --> 00:08:59,267
The field's inactive.

122
00:08:59,268 --> 00:09:03,840
Most of the gas has been distributed out in pipelines and sold to the market.

123
00:09:04,320 --> 00:09:09,840
There's about 20% of the gas left as a cushion at relatively low pressures to keep the system alive,

124
00:09:09,940 --> 00:09:12,480
to keep the caverns open, to keep the equipment running,

125
00:09:12,700 --> 00:09:16,380
while the company decides whether they want to apply to reopen it.

126
00:09:17,040 --> 00:09:22,960
Following the Hutchinson disaster, state laws were passed requiring salt caverns to be built and maintained

127
00:09:22,961 --> 00:09:24,800
with higher safety standards.

128
00:09:25,680 --> 00:09:29,060
There's also a concern that it happened once.

129
00:09:29,660 --> 00:09:32,240
We still don't know all of the things that went wrong.

130
00:09:32,380 --> 00:09:35,640
There's still maybe other pathways for gas that we've never discovered.

131
00:09:36,500 --> 00:09:40,720
And so I think there's always going to be a reasonable doubt in many people's minds

132
00:09:40,721 --> 00:09:46,740
about living uphill from a facility this large with this high-pressure gas.

133
00:09:46,741 --> 00:09:55,080
In 2004, three and a half years after the Hutchinson incident,

134
00:09:55,700 --> 00:09:58,180
natural gas stored in a salt cavern in Texas

135
00:09:58,181 --> 00:10:02,020
exploded and burned for a week before going out.

136
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:06,780
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

137
00:10:06,781 --> 00:10:20,781
On October 31st, 1984, around midnight, the tanker Puerto Rican was beginning a journey from San Francisco to New Orleans.

138
00:10:24,340 --> 00:10:30,400
It was plying its trade of calling on ports and harbors and would take chemicals into its tanks,

139
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:36,700
go to its destination, offload them, take on new chemicals, and finally end up in its next destination.

140
00:10:36,780 --> 00:10:43,720
the problem arose of course when it sailed out of the golden gate the evening of its last voyage

141
00:10:43,721 --> 00:10:50,720
guided out of port by a pilot boat the 632 foot long craft carried a crew of about 30

142
00:10:50,721 --> 00:10:59,880
and a cargo of nearly 92 000 barrels of lubrication oil and additives and 8 500 barrels of bunker fuel

143
00:10:59,881 --> 00:11:04,340
It was a clear, dark night. Everything looked great.

144
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:09,720
I was just about maybe eight hours away from finishing up my work and going on a 30-day vacation.

145
00:11:10,160 --> 00:11:13,320
The two vessels, pilot boat and tanker, were tied together.

146
00:11:14,240 --> 00:11:20,600
But at 3.30 a.m., as the pilot boat skipper was preparing to disengage the tanker and return to his vessel,

147
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:23,660
the night sky lit up with terrific intensity.

148
00:11:23,661 --> 00:11:30,700
I had my hand on the railing and I was just about ready to go down when I heard a click,

149
00:11:30,701 --> 00:11:32,059
a whoosh and a bang.

150
00:11:32,060 --> 00:11:37,360
I basically kind of remember the ship exploding, but I don't remember anything

151
00:11:37,361 --> 00:11:43,009
else till I was way up in the air. I must have been 200 feet up in the air.

152
00:11:43,010 --> 00:11:44,440
So as it was coming

153
00:11:44,441 --> 00:11:49,920
down, I actually saw the ship exploding in kaleidoscope form, was going click, click,

154
00:11:49,921 --> 00:11:52,960
It clicked like it's unbelievable, like a slideshow.

155
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:56,200
The Puerto Rican had turned into a fireball.

156
00:11:57,280 --> 00:12:00,640
Within minutes, the United States Coast Guard scrambled a rescue response.

157
00:12:01,940 --> 00:12:07,320
We got a report from a C-130 aircraft, which is one of our large four-engine aircraft.

158
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:09,920
It was about 15 miles off the coast.

159
00:12:10,340 --> 00:12:18,560
And that report told us that there was about a 40 to 50-foot plume of flame leaping up from the vessel.

160
00:12:18,561 --> 00:12:20,760
And so we knew it was very, very serious.

161
00:12:21,400 --> 00:12:24,900
The pilot survived along with a crewman who was badly burned.

162
00:12:25,760 --> 00:12:30,460
But a second crewman thrown overboard by the blast was never found.

163
00:12:33,200 --> 00:12:35,860
The explosion had blown the tanker in half.

164
00:12:36,820 --> 00:12:40,360
It spilled 1.4 million gallons of oil into the bay.

165
00:12:41,560 --> 00:12:45,180
The timing of the disaster created special challenges for the Coast Guard,

166
00:12:45,181 --> 00:12:47,620
which not only had to rescue survivors

167
00:12:47,621 --> 00:12:50,140
but would also try to clean up the spill.

168
00:12:50,620 --> 00:12:53,100
This explosion, of course, as I said, happened at night.

169
00:12:53,580 --> 00:12:56,380
So how much oil was in the water immediately we couldn't even see.

170
00:12:56,760 --> 00:12:59,880
But at first light we were able to see what was trailing from the vessel

171
00:12:59,881 --> 00:13:03,420
and we were very much at risk of losing the entire cargo

172
00:13:03,421 --> 00:13:05,780
which was about 4 million gallons.

173
00:13:07,680 --> 00:13:10,060
A few days later, during heavy weather

174
00:13:10,061 --> 00:13:12,740
the Coast Guard's worst fears were realized.

175
00:13:13,700 --> 00:13:18,560
The rear portion of the vessel sank about 25 miles off the coast of San Francisco,

176
00:13:19,460 --> 00:13:21,460
releasing even more oil into the bay.

177
00:13:22,260 --> 00:13:26,020
And it took with it about 8,000 barrels of fuel oil.

178
00:13:26,920 --> 00:13:31,380
And at the time, some of that oil leaked out and continued to leak for some time afterwards,

179
00:13:31,620 --> 00:13:33,160
about 20 barrels a day, I think.

180
00:13:33,720 --> 00:13:38,100
The Coast Guard's response involved specially equipped oil spill recovery vessels.

181
00:13:38,101 --> 00:13:45,100
These boats have floating barriers that funnel oil into tanks or large rubber bladders.

182
00:13:45,101 --> 00:13:50,100
The Coast Guard sent planes aloft to drop detergents on the oil to disperse it.

183
00:13:50,101 --> 00:13:57,100
In the weeks after the disaster, the Coast Guard's environmental specialists worked to mop up the spill.

184
00:13:57,101 --> 00:14:06,100
Simultaneously, Maritime Police, Coast Guard investigators, and private failure analysis contractors were looking for the cause of the disaster.

185
00:14:06,101 --> 00:14:11,100
They studied the remains of the tanker, which was in dry dock.

186
00:14:11,101 --> 00:14:21,100
A dependable feature of explosives and explosions is that objects move away from the center of an explosion,

187
00:14:21,101 --> 00:14:28,100
generally in the direction that when you plot a point of their initial position to their final position,

188
00:14:28,101 --> 00:14:31,100
they point back to what blew them away.

189
00:14:31,101 --> 00:14:34,420
This was not exactly rocket science because the ship split there.

190
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:37,800
So it left a pretty clear signature.

191
00:14:37,960 --> 00:14:42,280
So we knew the tank area where the explosion occurred.

192
00:14:43,380 --> 00:14:46,220
Investigators focused their efforts on tank number five.

193
00:14:47,380 --> 00:14:51,080
Curiously, that tank had carried nothing explosive for almost six years.

194
00:14:51,940 --> 00:14:54,300
But the blast had left something behind.

195
00:14:55,020 --> 00:14:55,720
Soot.

196
00:14:56,880 --> 00:15:00,720
Investigators believed the inky black substance might have held clues to the mystery.

197
00:15:01,100 --> 00:15:08,900
Looking down on the ship in dry dock, you see the most prominent feature is the folded back deck.

198
00:15:09,760 --> 00:15:12,340
It's black. It's carbon black.

199
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:14,200
That's soot.

200
00:15:15,140 --> 00:15:27,116
To determine the constituents in the soot, there are several investigatory tools that are very powerful in identifying for us the very molecular and atomic

201
00:15:27,117 --> 00:15:29,820
constituents that led to that soot.

202
00:15:29,821 --> 00:15:38,620
The most powerful tools we find in this endeavor are some combination of gas chromatography or mass spectrometry.

203
00:15:39,160 --> 00:15:47,340
What these tools basically do is you take a complex chemical mishmash like soot,

204
00:15:47,900 --> 00:15:52,700
and you want to break it into its constituents so you can identify them individually.

205
00:15:53,520 --> 00:15:57,940
The analysis revealed that one of the compounds involved was caustic soda.

206
00:15:57,941 --> 00:16:01,740
by itself caustic soda is not explosive

207
00:16:01,741 --> 00:16:05,180
but as investigators would learn

208
00:16:05,181 --> 00:16:07,280
it leaked from the holding tank

209
00:16:07,281 --> 00:16:09,160
combined with other elements

210
00:16:09,161 --> 00:16:11,220
and formed a dangerous brew

211
00:16:11,221 --> 00:16:14,080
investigators went inside

212
00:16:14,081 --> 00:16:15,920
and found a hole at the top of the tank

213
00:16:15,921 --> 00:16:18,160
a problem they believed

214
00:16:18,161 --> 00:16:19,920
that occurred because one of the welders

215
00:16:19,921 --> 00:16:20,660
who made the tank

216
00:16:20,661 --> 00:16:23,060
didn't seal the seam completely

217
00:16:23,061 --> 00:16:25,560
the hole burned through the tank

218
00:16:25,561 --> 00:16:29,200
was actually easier to find than you might think

219
00:16:29,201 --> 00:16:33,300
because large expanses of a tank wall are just flat, featureless.

220
00:16:34,760 --> 00:16:37,000
And if you're in this business very long,

221
00:16:37,001 --> 00:16:43,660
you recognize that problems in workmanship don't come up, typically,

222
00:16:43,960 --> 00:16:47,080
in long, straight areas that are easy to weld.

223
00:16:47,480 --> 00:16:50,340
Problems in workmanship usually manifest themselves

224
00:16:50,341 --> 00:16:54,680
in complex, cramped geometries around corners and struts

225
00:16:54,681 --> 00:16:59,880
in a place that's not so accessible and not so easy for the workman to see is work.

226
00:17:01,180 --> 00:17:07,520
The hole in the tank was enough to provide passage into a void area between Tank 5 and its neighbor 6.

227
00:17:09,080 --> 00:17:17,640
Eventually, the bilge space became filled with a soup of caustic soda from a preceding voyage.

228
00:17:17,641 --> 00:17:24,420
This caustic soda soup combined with a zinc coating used in the bilge area and formed hydrogen gas.

229
00:17:24,680 --> 00:17:29,320
Then the hydrogen exploded, ignited by a spark of unknown origin.

230
00:17:30,400 --> 00:17:37,860
If hydrogen finally builds up in concentration to its lower explosive limit, it will find an ignition source.

231
00:17:38,080 --> 00:17:39,740
Remember, this is the same stuff in the Hindenburg.

232
00:17:40,240 --> 00:17:43,260
So we have this incredible conjunction of events.

233
00:17:43,261 --> 00:17:57,261
And indeed, in our business, we see that major disasters are very often the result of a string of very low probability events that just all come together bad that day.

234
00:17:57,660 --> 00:18:01,232
The Puerto Rican tanker explosion had implications for the U.S.

235
00:18:01,233 --> 00:18:05,200
Coast Guard, which improved its response to hazardous material spills.

236
00:18:05,201 --> 00:18:11,320
And although authorities acknowledge that the explosion was a freak accident,

237
00:18:11,920 --> 00:18:15,520
the disaster did create change in the shipping industry.

238
00:18:15,521 --> 00:18:20,760
The Marine Board of Investigation suggested that there were preventative measures

239
00:18:20,761 --> 00:18:23,800
that could have been taken to prevent this explosion in the first place.

240
00:18:24,040 --> 00:18:26,700
Better inspection techniques, perhaps during the yard period,

241
00:18:26,820 --> 00:18:29,160
or other opportunities the vessel had to be inspected,

242
00:18:29,560 --> 00:18:32,660
may have found this gouge and could have been repaired.

243
00:18:33,620 --> 00:18:40,160
You've got to start looking for things like if volumes of cargo start disappearing in voyages,

244
00:18:40,360 --> 00:18:43,040
and in this particular case they were, in the case of the ship,

245
00:18:43,620 --> 00:18:47,720
this cannot go without some affirmative action.

246
00:18:48,400 --> 00:18:51,340
And indeed, it would be very straightforward to determine you've got a leak

247
00:18:51,341 --> 00:18:53,820
because cargo is showing up in the bilge water.

248
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,700
If those determinations had been made, then the leak would have been located.

249
00:18:56,900 --> 00:18:58,260
It just would have been a matter of time.

250
00:18:58,261 --> 00:19:05,940
As many as 126,000 gallons of caustic soda

251
00:19:05,941 --> 00:19:09,600
were estimated to have been lost in the bilge of the tanker Puerto Rican

252
00:19:09,601 --> 00:19:10,720
before it exploded.

253
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:16,040
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

254
00:19:19,100 --> 00:19:22,820
In 2003, the gambling town of Atlantic City, New Jersey

255
00:19:22,821 --> 00:19:24,280
was showing its age,

256
00:19:24,480 --> 00:19:26,700
and many believed it was in need of a facelift.

257
00:19:28,260 --> 00:19:32,420
The Tropicana organization stepped in with a new development,

258
00:19:32,920 --> 00:19:35,320
intended to lure more gamblers to the Jersey Shore.

259
00:19:36,900 --> 00:19:40,560
Construction began on a $245 million project,

260
00:19:41,140 --> 00:19:44,320
a new 18-story hotel next to the Tropicana Casino,

261
00:19:44,820 --> 00:19:48,360
as well as an attached 2,400-space parking structure.

262
00:19:49,580 --> 00:19:53,060
It was one of the biggest projects in Atlantic City in a decade.

263
00:19:54,060 --> 00:19:57,360
Local officials and Tropicana executives were saying,

264
00:19:57,361 --> 00:20:00,420
how big this is going to be for the area.

265
00:20:00,620 --> 00:20:02,960
It's going to be the largest hotel in the state of New Jersey.

266
00:20:03,120 --> 00:20:05,040
It's going to bring jobs to the area.

267
00:20:05,820 --> 00:20:10,160
It was of critical importance to the city and to the state and to the casino.

268
00:20:11,700 --> 00:20:17,200
It had the economic fortunes of workers and businesses tied to it.

269
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:22,900
Feeling the pressure, contractors put the Tropicana structure on a rush status

270
00:20:22,901 --> 00:20:25,220
to complete the work by March 2004.

271
00:20:25,221 --> 00:20:32,480
On the morning of October 30th, a work crew was eight parking levels up, preparing to pour concrete.

272
00:20:33,460 --> 00:20:37,540
On many construction sites, this is done by means of a crane-mounted hose.

273
00:20:38,420 --> 00:20:43,180
In the old days, workers would haul up concrete in buckets or in wheelbarrows.

274
00:20:43,300 --> 00:20:45,520
It's become much more modern these days.

275
00:20:45,660 --> 00:20:50,020
It's pumped. A pump takes it up through a tube and then out through a hose,

276
00:20:50,120 --> 00:20:53,080
so you could place it eight, nine floors high.

277
00:20:54,060 --> 00:20:56,500
Wet concrete puts great stress on structures.

278
00:20:57,440 --> 00:20:58,820
In the case of the Tropicana,

279
00:20:59,320 --> 00:21:03,400
engineers estimate that stress rose to 160 pounds per square foot

280
00:21:03,401 --> 00:21:07,100
on the framed-out floor area while the concrete was being poured.

281
00:21:08,060 --> 00:21:11,980
At about 10.40 in the morning, workers knew that something was wrong.

282
00:21:12,760 --> 00:21:15,080
They hear a noise and something shift.

283
00:21:15,580 --> 00:21:18,100
Some of the men have described it that they're just sort of...

284
00:21:18,101 --> 00:21:21,420
they're just sort of looking at each other like,

285
00:21:21,421 --> 00:21:23,400
Did you hear or feel that?

286
00:21:24,140 --> 00:21:28,440
And then everything begins to collapse and fall in.

287
00:21:29,960 --> 00:21:34,340
Within moments, five floor slabs collapsed progressively.

288
00:21:36,940 --> 00:21:41,540
They severed from a sheer wall that remained standing after the collapse.

289
00:21:42,360 --> 00:21:45,940
When it cracked like that, I just went straight down.

290
00:21:45,941 --> 00:21:52,060
And it's like, you know, flying like I'm just floating in the air and reaching, just trying to reach for something.

291
00:21:52,720 --> 00:22:00,440
Warren Webb rode the collapse down and was hospitalized with a severed hamstring, additional leg injuries, and a rotator cuff tear.

292
00:22:01,540 --> 00:22:07,400
Other workers were missing, however, and rescue personnel searched the collapsed structure for signs of life.

293
00:22:09,400 --> 00:22:15,520
When the last ambulance left the scene, the grim statistics were four workers killed.

294
00:22:15,940 --> 00:22:17,180
More than 20 injured.

295
00:22:17,740 --> 00:22:19,600
The reaction was intense.

296
00:22:20,180 --> 00:22:21,760
New stations carried it live.

297
00:22:22,180 --> 00:22:26,060
Family members whose husbands or wives were working here

298
00:22:26,061 --> 00:22:30,660
flocked to the scene to make sure that their loved ones got out alive.

299
00:22:31,800 --> 00:22:34,660
But soon after the tragedy, reports emerged

300
00:22:34,661 --> 00:22:38,020
that at the parking garage there had been early signs of trouble.

301
00:22:38,580 --> 00:22:44,200
We interviewed two workers who had been inside the garage weeks prior to the collapse,

302
00:22:44,201 --> 00:22:47,840
and what they had noticed were large cracks in the columns.

303
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:51,380
And they said they reported those large cracks to their supervisors.

304
00:22:52,280 --> 00:22:56,420
OSHA itself, in its press conference announcing its citations,

305
00:22:56,620 --> 00:23:02,820
indicated that there were reports of several areas of cracked concrete on the construction project.

306
00:23:03,040 --> 00:23:07,220
And OSHA indicated that this should have raised what OSHA described as red flags

307
00:23:07,221 --> 00:23:10,380
for the people involved in the construction process.

308
00:23:10,381 --> 00:23:18,600
In the days following the collapse, OSHA investigators and police sifted through the wreckage, looking for clues into how the structure failed.

309
00:23:19,520 --> 00:23:24,600
They knew the stresses created by wet concrete could push structures beyond their capabilities.

310
00:23:25,820 --> 00:23:32,520
And the amount of stress that it puts on the structure is enormous because it is when the structure will be stressed the most.

311
00:23:33,500 --> 00:23:38,520
Pumping concrete through a hose is common and considered safe if done with proper structural support.

312
00:23:38,521 --> 00:23:49,315
When the project began, iron workers on the lower floors were threading reinforcing steel, known as rebar, through the floor, and connecting the ends with additional

313
00:23:49,316 --> 00:23:54,680
pieces of rebar placed in the six vertical columns at the perimeter of the garage.

314
00:23:55,160 --> 00:23:57,820
That steel is literally getting tied together,

315
00:23:57,980 --> 00:23:59,460
and then when the concrete hardens,

316
00:23:59,540 --> 00:24:03,540
you have a substantial steel rods coming in from either direction,

317
00:24:03,660 --> 00:24:08,760
tied together, giving you a great structural member there that you could rely on.

318
00:24:09,360 --> 00:24:13,680
But then, according to OSHA and lawyers for the Tropicana victims,

319
00:24:14,380 --> 00:24:19,100
the project plans were changed to allow for the use of a different kind of rebar fabrication.

320
00:24:20,060 --> 00:24:26,840
Rebar mesh is a waffle weave of metal that is prefabricated, pre-cut, and simply dropped in place.

321
00:24:27,800 --> 00:24:34,360
Obviously, the more you can do things off-site and not in the field, the cheaper it's going to be.

322
00:24:34,680 --> 00:24:40,500
If you can eliminate rod setters tying pieces of rebar together and that's going to save you money,

323
00:24:40,980 --> 00:24:43,840
there's an economic incentive for companies to do that.

324
00:24:43,841 --> 00:24:52,000
According to OSHA investigators, the contractors also revised their plan for support beams, making them shallower and potentially weaker.

325
00:24:53,540 --> 00:24:59,180
Investigators say the unfortunate result was a structure that was substantially weaker than designed.

326
00:25:00,060 --> 00:25:03,460
OSHA concluded that the accident occurred for two basic reasons.

327
00:25:03,461 --> 00:25:10,380
That there was a lack of shoring and structural support, and that there was a lack of connections between the floors and the walls.

328
00:25:10,381 --> 00:25:16,940
Connecting horizontal steel to the vertical is a basic construction engineering design.

329
00:25:17,060 --> 00:25:17,760
It's very basic.

330
00:25:18,100 --> 00:25:22,320
Since the time of early man, once they started building structures,

331
00:25:22,460 --> 00:25:27,980
they knew that if you don't have the wall and the floor joined together,

332
00:25:28,640 --> 00:25:30,600
they're not going to be joined together for long.

333
00:25:31,080 --> 00:25:38,720
On April 29, 2004, OSHA fined the participating contractors $119,000

334
00:25:38,721 --> 00:25:42,440
and the contractors stated their intention to file appeals.

335
00:25:44,100 --> 00:25:47,060
Victims of the disaster believed the system failed them.

336
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:51,060
We believe that if this job had been adequately inspected,

337
00:25:51,200 --> 00:25:52,620
this accident would not have occurred.

338
00:25:53,140 --> 00:25:55,260
In the rush to build to a tight time frame,

339
00:25:56,100 --> 00:25:59,440
investigators believe a seemingly obvious principle of construction

340
00:25:59,441 --> 00:26:02,080
was ignored by the Tropicana's engineers.

341
00:26:04,120 --> 00:26:08,080
OSHA officials claim the walls and floors were not properly connected.

342
00:26:08,720 --> 00:26:17,140
That's why the OSHA director in discussing this catastrophe described it as a failure of basic engineering 101.

343
00:26:18,420 --> 00:26:26,980
According to federal statistics, a fall from one level to another is the most common cause of death on commercial building construction sites.

344
00:26:28,480 --> 00:26:31,640
Engineering disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

345
00:26:31,641 --> 00:26:45,641
In Russia's great northwest, from the White Sea and inlet to the Bering Sea, down to Lake Onega on the Finnish border,

346
00:26:46,880 --> 00:26:54,400
snow is on the ground for more than 160 days a year, and icebreakers are required to keep shipping lanes open in winter.

347
00:26:54,401 --> 00:27:02,040
This unforgiving landscape was an unwelcome home to an engineering project doomed to disaster.

348
00:27:05,500 --> 00:27:10,680
In 1932, Russia was in the brutal grip of Soviet communism.

349
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:21,320
The Soviet dictator, Joseph Stalin, was consolidating his power, rounding up enemies of the state in a process called collectivization.

350
00:27:21,321 --> 00:27:30,190
There had been mass arrests throughout the countryside and throughout the cities as well of people who didn't want to cooperate with the new order, who didn't want to

351
00:27:30,191 --> 00:27:32,220
collaborate with the collectivization.

352
00:27:33,120 --> 00:27:43,570
As a part of this process, the Soviet government identified people whom it considered to be rich peasants and then were either arrested or exiled to remote parts of

353
00:27:43,571 --> 00:27:44,271
Siberia.

354
00:27:44,272 --> 00:27:51,400
Stalin wanted to use the prisoners for propaganda purposes

355
00:27:51,401 --> 00:27:56,040
and decided to force them to work on grandiose public works projects.

356
00:27:56,720 --> 00:28:00,940
The first was the 140-mile-long White Sea Canal.

357
00:28:01,940 --> 00:28:05,180
It would allow for the strategic movement of Soviet ships

358
00:28:05,181 --> 00:28:08,160
and would create a new passage for commercial vessels.

359
00:28:09,300 --> 00:28:13,140
The canal was to extend from Povignets, at the northern end of Lake Onega,

360
00:28:13,141 --> 00:28:17,480
up to the White Sea with its important port city of Arkhangelsk.

361
00:28:17,880 --> 00:28:21,380
The route had a 229-foot difference in water level.

362
00:28:22,180 --> 00:28:26,600
This required a system of 19 locks to raise and lower water levels

363
00:28:26,601 --> 00:28:28,220
so that ships could pass.

364
00:28:30,220 --> 00:28:33,980
Stalin gave his engineers just 20 months to complete the job.

365
00:28:35,180 --> 00:28:37,540
Everyone involved with the project understood that

366
00:28:37,541 --> 00:28:40,560
when Stalin set a timetable, then you better stick to it.

367
00:28:40,561 --> 00:28:44,820
The project would begin what became known as the Gulag system,

368
00:28:45,400 --> 00:28:50,700
with workers living in nine camps along the route, working 16-hour days.

369
00:28:51,360 --> 00:28:56,040
The Gulag is a Soviet acronym, which means the State Administration of Camps.

370
00:28:56,500 --> 00:28:58,420
And the Gulag is simply a bureaucracy.

371
00:28:59,160 --> 00:29:03,580
It comes to be synonymous with the concentration camp system.

372
00:29:03,581 --> 00:29:13,760
they had hundreds of thousands of people who'd been jailed what should we do with them well

373
00:29:13,761 --> 00:29:19,800
we can't use them on farms let's start them on this grand project while the intended purpose

374
00:29:19,801 --> 00:29:25,940
of the white sea canal was both military and economic perhaps more important was its propaganda

375
00:29:25,941 --> 00:29:26,641
the value.

376
00:29:31,140 --> 00:29:36,340
There were films made, there were books written, there were newspaper articles done with interviewing

377
00:29:36,341 --> 00:29:40,600
people who would say things like, I came into the camp as a hardened criminal and I

378
00:29:40,601 --> 00:29:42,720
emerge now as a good Soviet person.

379
00:30:09,334 --> 00:30:20,256
It's a showpiece to prove to the world and to Soviet citizens that the socialist economy was better, stronger, and could do things more quickly and do things that

380
00:30:20,257 --> 00:30:23,762
It's a showpiece. It's a showpiece for Soviet power.

381
00:30:23,763 --> 00:30:25,920
people had only been able to do.

382
00:30:25,940 --> 00:30:32,183
dreamed of. But as work started, the engineering challenges only seemed to worsen.

383
00:30:32,184 --> 00:30:33,020
No geologic

384
00:30:33,021 --> 00:30:38,120
survey maps existed for large portions of the proposed canal, so engineers had no way

385
00:30:38,121 --> 00:30:41,470
of knowing soil conditions to help plan their excavations.

386
00:30:41,471 --> 00:30:43,260
They found to their dismay that

387
00:30:43,261 --> 00:30:48,080
workers would have to dig through 23 miles of solid granite to make the canal.

388
00:30:50,740 --> 00:30:55,840
Timber readily available in the forested region was the primary construction material,

389
00:30:55,940 --> 00:31:01,360
But in the interest of efficiency, any iron required was to be forged on site.

390
00:31:02,340 --> 00:31:05,880
Stalin insisted that the project stay on budget.

391
00:31:07,400 --> 00:31:13,920
One way of cutting costs was to have convicts be employed in all phases of the construction.

392
00:31:14,620 --> 00:31:20,560
Not just in terms of the labor, the unskilled labor, but also the skilled labor, meaning the engineers, the design.

393
00:31:20,660 --> 00:31:21,980
This was done by convict labor.

394
00:31:22,920 --> 00:31:26,800
The working conditions on the White Sea Canal were stunningly primitive.

395
00:31:27,460 --> 00:31:30,340
I've actually been to a museum up near the canal

396
00:31:30,341 --> 00:31:35,400
which preserves some of the tools that people used to work on it.

397
00:31:35,760 --> 00:31:38,120
And they include things like bits of metal,

398
00:31:38,860 --> 00:31:43,420
sort of stuck onto sticks, and that was what they used for hammers.

399
00:31:43,620 --> 00:31:45,380
There were hardly any machines used.

400
00:31:45,980 --> 00:31:49,340
Everything was done by hand, including all the breaking up of the rocks,

401
00:31:49,341 --> 00:31:53,220
all the digging, all the moving around of material and so on.

402
00:31:53,660 --> 00:31:56,180
Another cost-cutting measure was to reduce rations.

403
00:31:57,160 --> 00:32:00,040
In winter, they had to be housed and they had to be fed.

404
00:32:00,680 --> 00:32:02,200
They had very small rations.

405
00:32:02,940 --> 00:32:05,560
It was a very nasty experience.

406
00:32:07,940 --> 00:32:10,040
About 100,000 people worked on the project

407
00:32:10,041 --> 00:32:12,440
during the 30 months that it took to complete.

408
00:32:13,000 --> 00:32:16,920
Of these, about 25,000 people are thought to have died.

409
00:32:17,380 --> 00:32:19,120
It cost many lives.

410
00:32:19,340 --> 00:32:23,200
But Stalin's canal opened on May 28, 1933.

411
00:32:25,060 --> 00:32:28,840
But there was a hitch in the plan to make it a symbol of Soviet success.

412
00:32:29,700 --> 00:32:33,360
The White Sea Canal was practically useless.

413
00:32:33,640 --> 00:32:39,540
One problem is that in order to build the canal this cheaply,

414
00:32:39,940 --> 00:32:45,620
the decision is made to build it not as deeply as it should have been built.

415
00:32:45,621 --> 00:32:51,960
So instead of being built at a depth of 18 feet, it was built at a depth of between 10 and 12 feet.

416
00:32:52,680 --> 00:32:58,620
Most Soviet naval vessels had a draw of more than 12 feet, making passage through the canal impossible.

417
00:33:01,320 --> 00:33:04,700
Soviet engineers later learned they had made more disastrous decisions.

418
00:33:05,700 --> 00:33:11,020
The walls of the locks being made of wood crumbled, and the locks failed.

419
00:33:11,800 --> 00:33:13,960
Even the location turned out to be bad.

420
00:33:14,960 --> 00:33:18,000
The waters of the canal were frozen nearly half the year.

421
00:33:19,340 --> 00:33:22,260
Still, Stalin pretended the canal was a success.

422
00:33:22,860 --> 00:33:26,320
The fact that it didn't work very well was never widely advertised.

423
00:33:26,840 --> 00:33:28,920
This project was hailed as a tremendous success.

424
00:33:29,240 --> 00:33:30,820
Stalin himself was very proud of it.

425
00:33:33,100 --> 00:33:35,560
After World War II, the canal was rebuilt,

426
00:33:35,940 --> 00:33:38,660
with better walls and metal gates over the locks.

427
00:33:39,320 --> 00:33:41,980
But it's still too shallow for ocean-going vessels

428
00:33:41,981 --> 00:33:44,620
and receives light traffic today.

429
00:33:45,420 --> 00:33:49,540
I myself went up there and I saw maybe five or six ships

430
00:33:49,541 --> 00:33:50,900
in the course of an afternoon,

431
00:33:51,120 --> 00:33:52,640
and I talked to some of the people who worked there,

432
00:33:52,680 --> 00:33:55,820
and they said, oh, yes, we get the occasional barge coming through.

433
00:33:55,821 --> 00:33:59,760
But it's never been of major commercial use or major military use.

434
00:34:01,280 --> 00:34:04,240
A dictator's megalomania prompted poor engineering decisions

435
00:34:04,241 --> 00:34:06,860
that included forging ahead without a proper survey,

436
00:34:07,420 --> 00:34:10,520
working without proper tools or skilled personnel,

437
00:34:10,521 --> 00:34:18,140
And the Soviet supreme leader required that the truth about the disaster be concealed for decades.

438
00:34:19,360 --> 00:34:26,020
When Stalin died 20 years later, other gulag projects were abandoned as hopelessly impractical.

439
00:34:28,740 --> 00:34:36,120
Workers on the White Sea Canal project forged more than 1,000 tons of iron in handmade furnaces, partly made of wood.

440
00:34:37,080 --> 00:34:39,700
Engineering Disasters will return on Modern Marvels.

441
00:34:40,520 --> 00:34:46,880
It's abandoned, overgrown, choked with weeds.

442
00:34:48,620 --> 00:34:52,440
But amid rusting storage tanks and pipes that lead nowhere,

443
00:34:53,560 --> 00:34:59,200
discarded boxes of pesticide hold clues to one of the world's worst industrial disasters.

444
00:35:01,440 --> 00:35:07,900
It happened on Monday, December 3rd, 1984, when the night air carried a toxic cloud.

445
00:35:10,520 --> 00:35:17,980
It was 400 miles south of New Delhi, in a city with a name that would become synonymous with tragedy, Bhopal, India.

446
00:35:19,880 --> 00:35:25,620
In a shanty town surrounding a chemical manufacturing plant, residents had no warning.

447
00:35:26,240 --> 00:35:27,840
The death was virtually upon them.

448
00:35:32,080 --> 00:35:37,260
Sleepers were jolted awake, choking on poison and rubbing their stinging eyes.

449
00:35:37,261 --> 00:35:44,720
By first light, the cloud had cleared away, and many victims were doomed or already dead.

450
00:35:46,160 --> 00:35:53,260
Eventually, officials would report 3,800 fatalities, with 11,000 disabled with severe respiratory ailments.

451
00:35:54,880 --> 00:35:59,480
We don't believe there was an alarm system in place at Bhopal, not a public alarm system.

452
00:35:59,481 --> 00:36:04,280
I believe there was an alarm system within the factory itself,

453
00:36:04,420 --> 00:36:08,360
but there was no siren or public alarm that would alert the surrounding population

454
00:36:08,361 --> 00:36:11,900
to the threat of any potential hazard from the plant.

455
00:36:13,000 --> 00:36:18,040
Bhopal plant officials, however, say they sounded a toxic gas alarm for about five minutes.

456
00:36:19,860 --> 00:36:24,080
The Bhopal plant was run by an Indian subsidiary of Union Carbide,

457
00:36:24,640 --> 00:36:26,880
a U.S.-based multinational corporation.

458
00:36:27,880 --> 00:36:37,660
The plant manufactured a pesticide called 7 using an extremely toxic chemical, MIC, methyl isocyanate.

459
00:36:38,980 --> 00:36:43,460
It was MIC that escaped that night and poisoned residents in their sleep.

460
00:36:45,020 --> 00:36:53,060
In the 15 years the plant had been in operation before the disaster, there had been warning signs of trouble ahead, signs that may have been ignored.

461
00:36:53,061 --> 00:36:56,520
Accidents didn't happen each day

462
00:36:56,521 --> 00:36:59,300
But they became fairly routine

463
00:36:59,301 --> 00:37:02,240
Minor leaks of gas

464
00:37:02,241 --> 00:37:05,580
There had been leaks of phosgene gas

465
00:37:05,581 --> 00:37:06,860
Which is also deadly

466
00:37:06,861 --> 00:37:09,980
Although not as deadly perhaps as MIC

467
00:37:09,981 --> 00:37:14,140
After the methyl isocyanate tragedy

468
00:37:14,141 --> 00:37:16,360
Separate investigations were launched

469
00:37:16,361 --> 00:37:17,540
By the government of India

470
00:37:17,541 --> 00:37:19,220
By scores of journalists

471
00:37:19,221 --> 00:37:21,420
And also Union Carbide

472
00:37:22,380 --> 00:37:24,020
Conflicting theories emerged.

473
00:37:24,700 --> 00:37:29,820
One of the explanations is that the workers were flushing equipment with water.

474
00:37:30,340 --> 00:37:36,720
The water passed through a valve which was supposed to be closed but did not close all the way.

475
00:37:37,940 --> 00:37:42,700
Flushing with water, or water washing, was a normal maintenance procedure at Bhopal.

476
00:37:43,900 --> 00:37:48,500
At the time of the tragedy, the plant had been in standby mode for more than a month,

477
00:37:48,501 --> 00:37:53,800
not actively producing MIC because of the lack of demand for pest control in the winter.

478
00:37:54,300 --> 00:37:58,200
We know that the water washing started something like 10.30 p.m. at night

479
00:37:58,201 --> 00:38:00,640
and continued till approximately 11.30.

480
00:38:01,060 --> 00:38:03,440
We know that this water washing was taking place.

481
00:38:03,860 --> 00:38:08,340
Common sense tells you that is the strongest possibility of how the water actually entered the tank.

482
00:38:09,800 --> 00:38:13,860
Although routine, flushing the equipment can be a dangerous procedure

483
00:38:13,861 --> 00:38:18,900
because MIC reacts explosively when large amounts of it come into contact with water.

484
00:38:20,200 --> 00:38:25,900
The MIC tanks at Bhopal, three in all, were partially buried in the ground for stability.

485
00:38:27,040 --> 00:38:29,520
Each tank held about 45 tons of the chemical,

486
00:38:30,240 --> 00:38:34,180
making the plant among the largest MIC storage facilities in the world.

487
00:38:34,820 --> 00:38:39,860
When this reaction took place within that storage tank number 610,

488
00:38:40,060 --> 00:38:43,640
it took place with such an explosive violence and force.

489
00:38:43,860 --> 00:38:47,100
that the concrete tank literally lifted up,

490
00:38:47,840 --> 00:38:49,460
rupturing the concrete casing,

491
00:38:50,000 --> 00:38:52,040
and broke through the surface.

492
00:38:53,100 --> 00:38:55,140
The Indian government's investigation

493
00:38:55,141 --> 00:38:57,180
found that when the MIC reacted,

494
00:38:57,900 --> 00:38:59,740
27 tons of poison vapor

495
00:38:59,741 --> 00:39:02,180
and 14 tons of reaction byproducts

496
00:39:03,180 --> 00:39:05,580
were sent into the air for at least 90 minutes.

497
00:39:06,540 --> 00:39:08,420
Indian government investigators believed

498
00:39:08,421 --> 00:39:09,760
that a Bhopal plant worker

499
00:39:09,761 --> 00:39:11,980
failed to follow a safety procedure

500
00:39:11,981 --> 00:39:17,020
and didn't insert a piece of metal called a slip blind into a crucial valve.

501
00:39:17,780 --> 00:39:24,040
A slip blind would slide in between basically a solid wall in the pipe.

502
00:39:24,041 --> 00:39:27,980
It would be not possible for water to get past a slip blind.

503
00:39:28,600 --> 00:39:32,280
The Indian investigators also believed that the reaction was worsened

504
00:39:32,281 --> 00:39:36,120
because valves were poorly maintained and became clogged.

505
00:39:36,780 --> 00:39:40,360
These valves would have allowed the water to drain harmlessly away from the tank.

506
00:39:41,980 --> 00:39:47,020
They also pointed out that the cooling unit that serviced tank 610 was turned off,

507
00:39:47,940 --> 00:39:50,920
making the MIC inside even more reactive.

508
00:39:52,560 --> 00:39:58,080
In contrast, Union Carbides investigators came up with a different explanation for the Bhopal disaster.

509
00:39:59,800 --> 00:40:02,860
While they too believed the core problem was water contamination,

510
00:40:03,760 --> 00:40:08,020
they theorized that a disgruntled employee introduced water into the tank.

511
00:40:08,021 --> 00:40:13,180
This is exactly the kind of candidate who would have committed a particular act,

512
00:40:14,380 --> 00:40:16,700
intending one result and yet causing another,

513
00:40:16,880 --> 00:40:24,160
in the sense of intending to cause mischief or spoiling a batch of MIC by introducing water

514
00:40:24,161 --> 00:40:28,040
and yet not fully appreciating the consequences of his act.

515
00:40:29,800 --> 00:40:33,080
Journalists pointed to a larger systematic pattern of failures

516
00:40:33,081 --> 00:40:37,500
concerning the plant's site location, design, and day-to-day operations.

517
00:40:38,020 --> 00:40:45,540
A lot of people want to reduce this to some guy opened a valve and he shouldn't have.

518
00:40:45,840 --> 00:40:47,380
But that's not what happened.

519
00:40:47,880 --> 00:40:52,220
First of all, this whole plant was designed incorrectly.

520
00:40:52,221 --> 00:41:02,120
It was a time bomb waiting to explode because, number one, methyl isocyanide, a very deadly chemical, was used.

521
00:41:02,121 --> 00:41:09,160
The other decision that was made that's very crucial is MIC was stored in very large tanks.

522
00:41:09,740 --> 00:41:19,400
Therefore, if something went wrong, you had a massive disaster instead of a bad accident, but not a massive catastrophe.

523
00:41:20,540 --> 00:41:30,040
A comprehensive review of Bhopal investigations was initiated by a group of chemical companies, including Dow Chemical, which acquired Union Carbide in 1999.

524
00:41:30,041 --> 00:41:33,720
the group called the Center for Chemical Process Safety

525
00:41:33,721 --> 00:41:35,700
emphasized in its review

526
00:41:35,701 --> 00:41:38,500
that crucial equipment at the plant was not working

527
00:41:38,501 --> 00:41:41,540
because of poor maintenance or was just not turned on.

528
00:41:42,040 --> 00:41:44,460
The equipment included a device called a scrubber

529
00:41:44,461 --> 00:41:46,840
that could have chemically destroyed the MIC gases

530
00:41:46,841 --> 00:41:48,300
as they spewed from the tank.

531
00:41:48,880 --> 00:41:50,600
The scrubber was not operating that evening.

532
00:41:51,020 --> 00:41:51,800
It could have been.

533
00:41:51,940 --> 00:41:53,720
It was possible to have turned it on,

534
00:41:53,880 --> 00:41:56,320
but during the course of the event,

535
00:41:56,800 --> 00:41:58,520
the operators did not turn it on.

536
00:41:58,521 --> 00:42:04,180
Past the scrubber, there was also a process flare, which could have burned off the MIC that was being emitted.

537
00:42:04,740 --> 00:42:07,320
But also the process flare was not operating that day.

538
00:42:07,780 --> 00:42:14,520
So there was a number of safeguards that could have been and should have been in place, but they were not.

539
00:42:14,820 --> 00:42:21,620
The Center for Chemical Process Safety's review suggested that the disaster proceeded from a cascading series of failures,

540
00:42:22,240 --> 00:42:24,060
originating in the way the plant was run.

541
00:42:24,061 --> 00:42:36,845
You have to go back to the root cause, and in a case like this, I believe it would be typical to find that there was some kind of management failure that sits at the

542
00:42:36,846 --> 00:42:38,860
root cause of the problem.

543
00:42:39,000 --> 00:42:44,460
That may not be the only root cause, but it certainly is likely to be one of them.

544
00:42:44,461 --> 00:42:52,400
If any one of these measures, for example, the design of the plant, the design of the safety systems,

545
00:42:53,060 --> 00:42:58,980
the effort to maintain the plant, the effort to turn on the safety devices,

546
00:42:59,220 --> 00:43:01,800
if any one of these had been done,

547
00:43:02,480 --> 00:43:11,100
it would have contributed greatly to minimizing the kind of damage, death, and destruction that resulted from the gas leaks.

548
00:43:11,101 --> 00:43:16,540
Union Carbide paid a $470 million settlement to the government of India.

549
00:43:17,360 --> 00:43:24,260
But legal delays, including identifying some of the victims, have slowed the payout rate to a crawl.

550
00:43:26,740 --> 00:43:31,120
The release of the poison gas over Bhopal became an even greater disaster

551
00:43:31,121 --> 00:43:34,880
because of the close proximity of slums built up around the plant.

552
00:43:34,881 --> 00:43:42,800
Some health investigators estimate that since the disaster, as many as 50,000 people have

553
00:43:42,801 --> 00:43:48,120
sustained significant respiratory injuries from exposure to airborne and waterborne poisons.

554
00:43:50,820 --> 00:43:55,180
Bhopal has forced chemical plant designers to find ways to safely store MIC.

555
00:43:57,320 --> 00:44:02,500
Today, plants that use MIC employ auxiliary emergency dump tanks.

556
00:44:02,501 --> 00:44:07,840
These can be used to divert large amounts of the chemical away from contaminated areas.

557
00:44:09,400 --> 00:44:13,780
Two decades later, the city of Bhopal is still a toxic site,

558
00:44:14,180 --> 00:44:18,400
with scientists documenting widespread contamination of groundwater supplies.

559
00:44:19,760 --> 00:44:23,780
It is a tragic legacy to an incident that experts acknowledge

560
00:44:23,781 --> 00:44:27,320
was the worst industrial disaster in history.

